\title{Notation of generalized elements}
\author{hsuchingchung}
\import{macros-subobj}


\p{ Let \CCat be a category. In general, for an object #{A} in \CCat, we can not talk about what is an \em{element} of #{A}. But from the categorical semantic of type theory or in the lens of Yoneda lemma (do not worry if you did not heard about them), it seems to be a great idea to consider \strong{generalized elements} of #{A}.
}

\transclude{0002}

\p{The notation of generalized elements makes reasoning about objects like working with "sets." For the reader who knows the Yoneda embedding, it can be viewed as a converter sending an object #{A} to its generalized elements #{\HomC(-, A)} grouped by contexts, where #{\HomC(-, A)} denotes the [contravariant hom functor](000C) of #{A}. The following exercise gives us more intuition about the generalized element notation:}

\p{We use #{f g} to denote the composition #{f ∘ g} of morphisms.}

